= Situated Activity  [SA-01] =

//What a Stakeholder currently does, in the conditions they actually do it in. The empirical base everything else rests on.//

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-01
|Name|Brushing teeth
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature and group reasoning only - no residents, care staff or family have been interviewed or observed by this project, so this is provisional.

Rogers et al. (1999) describe morning care routines, including oral hygiene, of nursing-home residents with dementia and the graduated prompting staff use. Mihailidis et al. (2008) break a comparable stepwise ADL (handwashing) into steps for prompting people with dementia.

Longitudinal study about recall of everyday task [[https:~~/~~/pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11997490/>>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11997490/]]

Analysis of logical memory recall with Alzheimer's [[https:~~/~~/pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12597076/>>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12597076/]]
)))
|Goal|Maintain oral hygiene as part of the resident's normal morning routine, with as much independence as possible
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
ST-01: Resident with dementia (performs the activity)

ST-02: Care home staff member (supports and prompts when needed)
)))
|Trigger|Morning routine, after waking, washing and/or breakfast
|Constituent Tasks|(((
~1. Recognize that is time to brush teeth

2. Go to the bathroom/sink

3. Locate toothbrush and toothpaste

4. Pick up toothbrush

5. Open toothpaste

6. Put toothpaste on toothbrush

7. Brush all teeth

8. Rinse/spit

9. Put toothbrush and toothpaste away
)))
|Context|Resident's bathroom in a dementia care home usually during a morning/evening care routine. Care staff may be nearby, the resident's cognitive abilities and need for assistance can vary.
|Frequency and duration|Twice daily approximately 2-5 minutes per episode. The duration is a group estimate, not measured.
|Tools and artifacts|Toothbrush, toothpaste, sink, cup, mirror, towels, possible visual prompts/hints used by the care home 
|Existing workarounds|(((
- Verbally reminding the resident to brush their teeth

- Placing toothbrush in front of them

- Putting toothpaste onto the toothbrush

- Demonstrating the brushing action

- Breaking the activity into verbal instructions

- Physically assisting the resident
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
- Forgetting to start tooth brushing

- Losing track of the sequence

- Not recognising an object

- Performing a single step over and over again

- Stopping before completing the whole activity
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01 (supported by ST-02); illustrated-by → PS-01. Goal field grounds → OBJ-01 (§2.3).

= Situated Activity  [SA-02] =

//What a Stakeholder currently does, in the conditions they actually do it in. The empirical base everything else rests on.//

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-02
|Name|Taking prescribed medication
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature and group reasoning only - no residents, care staff or family have been interviewed or observed by this project, so this is provisional.

While, Duane, Beanland & Koch (2013), “Medication management: the perspectives of people with dementia and family carers.”

Longitudinal study about recall of everyday task [[https:~~/~~/pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11997490/>>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11997490/]]

Analysis of logical memory recall with Alzheimer's [[https:~~/~~/pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12597076/>>https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12597076/]]

Scope note: this activity is carried by UC02, deferred to a later cycle (see §2.3 Open item O-13).
)))
|Goal|Take the right medication at the right time, understanding what it is for, without needing to be reminded repeatedly
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
ST-01: Resident with dementia (performs the activity)

ST-02: Care home employee (prepares the medication and reminds the resident)
)))
|Trigger|Morning routine, after waking, washing and/or breakfast - depending on the needs of the patient/type of medication
|Constituent Tasks|(((
~1. Recognize that it is time to take medication

2. Identify which medicine/s should be taken

3. Go to where the medicines are stored/located 

4. Locate the medicine container

5. Open the medicine container

6. Put a designated amount of pills in the mouth

7. Locate cup of water

8. Swallow pills with the help of the water

9. Confirm that task was completed
)))
|Context|Care home, morning, staff present
|Frequency and duration|Daily, or as often as the prescription says. Duration 2-5 minutes per episode. The duration is a group estimate, not measured.
|Tools and artifacts|Glass, water, medication: pills, syringe, suppository, cream/ointment, food (when needs to be taken with food or to mask the taste)
|Existing workarounds|Post-it note with instructions, pill organizer, reminder app, reminder by caretaker in person, placing the medication in a visible spot, delegate an employee whose task is to assist with taking medication.
|Observed breakdowns|(((
- The resident has forgotten what type of medication they need to take, or has forgotten that they need to take medication entirely.

- The post-it note with instructions is missed.

- Medication has run out and was not refilled on time.

- The pill organizer (which medication on which day at which time) was not refilled or was refilled incorrectly by accident.

- The care home staff member is overloaded and did not check in with the resident on time.
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01 (supported by ST-02); illustrated-by → PS-02. Goal field grounds → UC02 (later cycle).

= Situated Activity  [SA-03] =

//What a Stakeholder currently does, in the conditions they actually do it in. The empirical base everything else rests on.//

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-03
|Name|Morning care round
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature and group reasoning only - no residents, care staff or family have been interviewed or observed by this project, so this is provisional.

Constructed from the care worker's part in PS-01 and PS-02 and from HP-02. Rogers et al. (1999) describe nursing-home morning care routines for residents with dementia, and found that graduated prompting took considerably more caregiver time than usual care.
)))
|Goal|Get every resident on the round through their morning care safely and in time, giving each the support they actually need, and know afterwards what each resident needed so care can continue at handover
|Performing Actor(s)|ST-02: Care home staff member
|Trigger|Start of the morning shift
|Constituent Tasks|(((
~1. Read the handover notes from the previous shift

2. Go from resident to resident: wake them, and assist with washing and dressing where needed

3. Prompt each resident through personal-care activities such as tooth brushing (SA-01)

4. Hand out medication and remind residents to take it (SA-02)

5. Return to residents who stalled or did not finish

6. Record the care given and hand over at the end of the shift
)))
|Context|One care worker is responsible for several residents during the busy morning period, under time pressure and with frequent interruptions. Residents' needs for support vary from day to day.
|Frequency and duration|Every morning shift. 5 - 8 residents per care worker, 30 - 60 minutes per round. The duration is a group estimate, not measured.
|Tools and artifacts|Handover notes and care record, medication list or trolley, residents' own personal-care items
|Existing workarounds|(((
- Preparing items in advance, such as putting toothpaste on the toothbrush (SA-01)

- Verbal reminders given in passing while attending to another resident

- Delegating one employee to handle medication for the whole round (SA-02)
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
- Having to return repeatedly to the same resident to prompt the next step (PS-01, PS-02)

- Being unable to attend to two residents at once, so one of them waits

- Prompting step by step takes considerable caregiver time (Rogers et al., 1999)

- Checking in on a resident too late because the care worker is overloaded (SA-02)
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-02; illustrated-by → PS-01, PS-02. Goal field grounds → OBJ-06 (§2.3).
